Andy: I suppose like anything there are many&nbsp;
different ways for actors to be quote unquote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:35.040
good or bad and I think you can I've started to&nbsp;
think about them a little bit like athletes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.240
some work harder than others&nbsp;
some have some natural talent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:49.760
but there are also specialty kind&nbsp;
of approaches like let's say like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:58.160
quirky actor like a specialty three-point shooter&nbsp;
it doesn't mean that this person is good at all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:06.160
the things but cast correctly casting I guess is&nbsp;
really part of it if they're in the right spot you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.240
put them in a position to succeed so one person&nbsp;
can really shine if they're in the right role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:19.040
and it's a well-written role of course whereas&nbsp;
that same person you could see on a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:25.840
set on a different day in a different role and&nbsp;
you're like I don't know about that like it's so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:34.880
there are a number of factors that said every once&nbsp;
in a while and I would not know how to to quantify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.640
it but every once in a while I find myself behind&nbsp;
the camera and I'm watching something I'm like oh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:47.840
this person's going to be around forever like you&nbsp;
there's just something I mean I guess back in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:55.200
day you would think of that like oh they've got&nbsp;
it I hesitate to put it that way but that said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.800
I have seen that I've seen actors were like oh&nbsp;
like that's me I had I had the good fortune of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:10.640
working with Matt Damon on on a tiny little like&nbsp;
a youtube thing I watched George Clooney work and

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:19.120
they are so prepared and so comfortable there's&nbsp;
like a confidence that they carry with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:26.000
they treated everybody around them phenomenally&nbsp;
well they were very loose but then when it came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.000
time to work they could snap right in&nbsp;
you could tell they had done the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:37.280
so all all of this stuff whereas other actors&nbsp;
come they don't know their lines and so I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:44.320
part of it is just preparation truly and on&nbsp;
set that comes across as like wow they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.240
really talented but really it's just that they&nbsp;
put in the work so I think that's part of it but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:59.280
I often find myself surprised when I'm on set&nbsp;
and it's time to do a scene and this person's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.280
been around and then all of a sudden the camera's&nbsp;
rolling and this like they morph into something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:12.560
else so I don't know where some of these things&nbsp;
come from I know some of it is work it's prep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.920
but some of it that maybe there is just&nbsp;
a natural thing I don't know I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.880
it's a little bit of magic I think I don't&nbsp;
know I continue to wonder the same thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.720
yeah what other qualities are you looking&nbsp;
for in an actor it's interesting so for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:36.320
because I have no power over choosing an actor in&nbsp;
my position as a cinematographer the actors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.880
chosen so whoever's there like I'm&nbsp;
gonna do whatever I can do with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:52.240
there are little treats that some actors bring&nbsp;
there are some actors who just physically their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:59.680
eyes will catch a light really well they have&nbsp;
like big or big brown eyes let's say and they just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.800
almost no matter where they are they'll always&nbsp;
catch a little bit of light and then some actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.000
their eyes are a little like deeper&nbsp;
I think I have kind of smallish eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.320
and it can get tricky because like an eye&nbsp;
light can go a long way when you're lighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.000
somebody in terms of creating an empathy there's&nbsp;
something really magical about an highlight and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.400
some actors it's hard to get the eye light you're&nbsp;
like trying to find the spot and some of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:34.720
won't take it so I mean that's a that's a purely&nbsp;
physical thing that they have no control over so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.000
there are like treats like that sometimes I'm like&nbsp;
all this and skin tones different actors they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.000
you're just like oh this like takes the the warmth&nbsp;
really well so there are little things like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:57.120
but a I mean I guess as with most people on&nbsp;
a set there really is to somebody being kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.920
and professional being prepared that's a actually&nbsp;
preparation is maybe one of the biggest things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:10.800
because we I've a number of times in my life we&nbsp;
I have with the director we plan this shot where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.720
oh they're going to take them out the door we're&nbsp;
going to dolly all the way down and then they sit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.960
here and then they're going to interact with this&nbsp;
person and get in their car and go and then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:25.440
actor can't get through their lines and you have&nbsp;
to re-approach the entire scene and do it because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.960
somebody doesn't know their lines and sometimes&nbsp;
that's hard because they have a ton of lines and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.160
that's brutal I wouldn't be able to do that but&nbsp;
sometimes it's a lack of preparation so I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.960
yeah I suppose for all the all&nbsp;
the random things I just named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:48.720
I want a prepared actor somebody is taking it&nbsp;
seriously because like it it affects my ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:55.680
to do a good job if somebody comes in unprepared&nbsp;
so I think something as basic as that is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:03.280
all you can ask from somebody is don't be a dick&nbsp;
and be prepared that's that goes a long way so if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.640
someone is not prepared then you're doing numerous&nbsp;
retakes people are reading them their lines it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.600
constant yeah there's there's constant like you&nbsp;
have to reset you have to take it all in pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.960
and if they can only get a few lines at a time it&nbsp;
means you have to cut to something else because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.880
in the edit you need when they're when they're&nbsp;
resetting you have to cut so it's forcing your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.480
hand in an edit and you have to rethink the&nbsp;
way you're building a scene which can mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:36.560
this is not the way we intended or this isn't this&nbsp;
isn't going to feel the way we want this to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:43.904
but you have to because and and look I say&nbsp;
that as though it's really easy that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:51.920
doing a shoot especially if there's one or two&nbsp;
lead characters and they have to memorize lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:59.680
for 15 weeks every day and scripts are being&nbsp;
rewritten it is a big ask that is a big load&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.280
that these actors are carrying like I really I&nbsp;
empathize with them it's really hard acting is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.880
I know we all tend to think of it as&nbsp;
like oh celebrity and you're famous and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:16.320
it is brutal it is hard work especially if&nbsp;
you're on a tv show scripts are changing you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:24.080
tons of dialogue and then on top of that you have&nbsp;
all these people whispering in your ear I have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:32.400
gauge when we do it let's say we're shooting&nbsp;
a scene and an actor on top of all their lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:37.280
maybe a director gives them direct oh no this part&nbsp;
don't forget you need to be sad in this little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:43.520
thing but then come back up but then for me I&nbsp;
maybe need an actor to land in a certain spot and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:50.800
maybe they haven't and I will have to gauge going&nbsp;
back to reading the room if they seem overwhelmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:56.640
I won't give them that note on the first take I&nbsp;
will like let them get a little more comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:02.000
and then if I can do anything on my side to adjust&nbsp;
maybe we tweak a light maybe I move my camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.280
then i'll do that otherwise I'm waiting until&nbsp;
they're comfortable and then as gently as I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:12.880
just like hey don't forget like after you get&nbsp;
up from the dinner table I need you to land in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:20.080
this area and I have to be very careful because&nbsp;
I am lower priority you want the performance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:24.000
to work if the performance doesn't work&nbsp;
it doesn't matter how pretty my light is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:31.760
you want the performance to lead so for me I have&nbsp;
to make sure an actor is comfortable and that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:37.840
feel comfortable with me and then gauging when&nbsp;
they're too overwhelmed or it's okay now to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:44.720
them a no or sometimes some directors prefer me&nbsp;
to give notes sometimes I go to the director and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:49.440
I say hey you need to remind them that they&nbsp;
have to get to this area and things like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:54.640
so there are lots of little things that actors&nbsp;
are constantly being bombarded with it's it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:02.000
so I think for for a cinematographer or a camera&nbsp;
operator it's really important to have empathy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:08.240
and realize that these actors even if you don't&nbsp;
like them they are carrying a lot and you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:14.720
to protect them and their space their head space&nbsp;
and the physical space that you're working in yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:18.480
that's why I always and I've said this numerous&nbsp;
times when people say oh well so-and-so doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.800
want to hang out in between takes and they just&nbsp;
go back into their dressing room I think that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:27.840
would need that time oh man I would not I would&nbsp;
definitely not hang out in between takes like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:35.040
there's no way there's just so much and people&nbsp;
especially if an actor has any kind of celebrity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:40.560
people get a little googly eyed and a little&nbsp;
and they want to interact they want to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:46.560
to go home to their spouse and just be like I was&nbsp;
talking with George Clooney about chicken today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:52.160
but they are there's so much going&nbsp;
on for them so I do my best to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:58.320
like just stay out of their way because there's&nbsp;
there's really a lot of people are really pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:03.520
at actors attentions same with directors&nbsp;
too but it does they don't have usually that&nbsp;&nbsp;

